## Goal
How do the six project samples cluster in a PCA of gene counts, with points coloured by their condition (untreated or treated)?

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. In the `gars-bio` environment, use Python with pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn, and matplotlib. Read the gene count TSV and design CSV; match count columns to `sample_id` values and use the design's `condition` for point colours. Confirm that exactly the six expected samples (A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3) are matched.
2. Treat the count matrix as genes by samples. Remove genes with zero total count, convert each sample's counts to counts per million (CPM), apply `log2(CPM + 1)`, then center each gene across samples. Run scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=2)` on the transposed centered matrix (samples by genes), with default solver and no random state needed for this full deterministic decomposition. Do not scale genes to unit variance.
3. Save the two-coordinate PCA table, including sample ID, condition, PC1, and PC2, as `results/pca_coordinates.csv`. Save a scatter plot of PC1 versus PC2 with points coloured and labelled by condition and sample ID as `results/pca_by_condition.png`; label axes with the variance explained by each component. All outputs are written under this analysis directory; inputs remain read-only.

